        "id": "hHyz-9iHx8sH"
      },
      "source": [
        "### Reading the first rows\n",
        "\n",
        "- The typical miss is about **$50,000 on a $180,000 house** — a 28% error. Serviceable, not impressive.\n",
        "- **Log price made it worse.** Surprising? Think about the shape: log price against a *raw* population count says that going from 300 people to 600 moves price by the same amount as going from 30,000 to 30,300. That is the wrong shape, and a few extreme neighbourhoods produce wild predictions once we exponentiate.\n",
        "- Train R² and test R² are close: OLS with 16 features and 16,000 rows is not overfitting. The problem is the *shape*."
      ],
      "id": "hHyz-9iHx8sH"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "KKqt4bM-x8sH"
      },
      "source": [
        "### Adjusting skewness: log the features too\n",
        "\n",
        "The four count columns and the ratios are skewed by a factor of 3–5. Logging them *and* the target gives the economist's favourite model — **log-log**, where every coefficient is an elasticity: a 1% change in income moves price by β%. Same shape on both sides of the equation.\n",
        "\n",
        "From here on, every model uses this preprocessing. (Trees won't care — a log is a monotone relabelling of the cut points — but it costs nothing.)"
      ],
      "id": "KKqt4bM-x8sH"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "_CtZ5Mz0x8sH",
        "outputId": "3d1da9ba-3a9b-42b4-a0ed-fd9c03df00a1"
      },
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "execute_result",
          "metadata": {},
          "execution_count": null,
          "data": {
            "text/plain": "                   target test MAE ($) test RMSE ($) test R² train R²\nOLS, raw price      price       49,645        69,127   0.635    0.658\nOLS, log price  log price       52,060        90,690   0.372    0.414\nOLS, log-log    log price       45,938        68,647   0.640    0.673",
            "text/html": "<div>\n<style scoped>\n    .dataframe tbody tr th:only-of-type {\n        vertical-align: middle;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe tbody tr th {\n        vertical-align: top;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe thead th {\n        text-align: right;\n    }\n</style>\n<table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"text-align: right;\">\n      <th></th>\n      <th>target</th>\n      <th>test MAE ($)</th>\n      <th>test RMSE ($)</th>\n      <th>test R²</th>\n      <th>train R²</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <th>OLS, raw price</th>\n      <td>price</td>\n      <td>49,645</td>\n      <td>69,127</td>\n      <td>0.635</td>\n      <td>0.658</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>OLS, log price</th>\n      <td>log price</td>\n      <td>52,060</td>\n      <td>90,690</td>\n      <td>0.372</td>\n      <td>0.414</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>OLS, log-log</th>\n      <td>log price</td>\n      <td>45,938</td>\n      <td>68,647</td>\n      <td>0.640</td>\n      <td>0.673</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n</div>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "source": [
        "SKEWED = [\"total_rooms\", \"total_bedrooms\", \"population\", \"households\", \"median_income\",\n",
        "          \"rooms_per_household\", \"people_per_household\"]\n",
        "\n",
        "def log_skewed(d):\n",
        "    d = d.copy()\n",
        "    for c in SKEWED:\n",
        "        d[c] = np.log1p(d[c])\n",
        "    return d\n",
        "\n",
        "preprocess = make_pipeline(\n",
        "    FunctionTransformer(add_ratios),\n",
        "    FunctionTransformer(log_skewed),\n",
        "    ColumnTransformer([\n",
        "        (\"num\", SimpleImputer(strategy=\"median\"), NUM),\n",
        "        (\"cat\", OneHotEncoder(handle_unknown=\"ignore\"), CAT),\n",
        "    ]),\n",
        ")\n",
        "ols = report(\"OLS, log-log\", LinearRegression())\n",
        "show()"
      ],
      "id": "_CtZ5Mz0x8sH"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "D-L9oINox8sH"
      },
      "source": [
        "### The shape was the problem\n",
        "\n",
        "Log on both sides: the typical miss drops from about $50,000 to about **$46,000**, and R² is back where it was. The \"adjust skewness\" step is not a ritual — it is choosing a functional form, and a linear model lives or dies by it."
      ],
      "id": "D-L9oINox8sH"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "3mlLbOUux8sH"
      },
      "source": [
        "### 6 · More features, and the problem of too many\n",
        "\n",
        "OLS is a straight line in every feature. One way to bend it: **polynomial features** — every square and every pairwise product. Eleven numeric columns at degree 2 become 81 columns.\n",
        "\n",
        "That is fine with 16,000 rows. It is *not* fine with 300. Let's see what happens when the rows run out."
      ],
      "id": "3mlLbOUux8sH"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "78WKywX_x8sH",
        "outputId": "fc41fc88-98e8-4ffd-9e44-e44ff41a2dfd"
      },
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "stream",
          "name": "stdout",
          "text": "OLS    degree-2 polynomial, 300 rows:  features = 81  train R² = 0.814  test MAE = $798,043\nridge  degree-2 polynomial, 300 rows:  features = 81  train R² = 0.767  test MAE = $46,779\n"
        }
      ],
      "source": [
        "poly_prep = make_pipeline(\n",
        "    FunctionTransformer(add_ratios),\n",
        "    FunctionTransformer(log_skewed),\n",
        "    ColumnTransformer([(\"num\", make_pipeline(SimpleImputer(strategy=\"median\"), StandardScaler(),\n",
        "                                             PolynomialFeatures(degree=2, include_bias=False)), NUM),\n",
        "                       (\"cat\", OneHotEncoder(handle_unknown=\"ignore\"), CAT)]),\n",
        ")\n",
        "small = X_train.sample(300, random_state=1).index   # pretend we only had 300 neighbourhoods\n",
        "\n",
        "for name, model in [(\"OLS\", LinearRegression()), (\"ridge\", Ridge(alpha=10))]:\n",
        "    pipe = make_pipeline(poly_prep, model).fit(X_train.loc[small], y_train.loc[small])   # raw price, to keep the damage finite\n",
        "    print(f\"{name:6s} degree-2 polynomial, 300 rows:  \"\n",
        "          f\"features = {pipe[:-1].transform(X_train.loc[small]).shape[1]}  \"\n",
        "          f\"train R² = {r2_score(y_train.loc[small], pipe.predict(X_train.loc[small])):.3f}  \"\n",
        "          f\"test MAE = ${mean_absolute_error(y_test, pipe.predict(X_test)):,.0f}\")"
      ],
      "id": "78WKywX_x8sH"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "cHQyui4sx8sH"
      },
      "source": [
        "### What just happened\n",
        "\n",
        "With 81 features and 300 rows, OLS fits the training data well — and its test error is **sixteen times worse** than the OLS we fit on all 16,000 rows. Module 2's overfitting, back in a new costume: not a wiggly curve this time, but a plane with too many knobs and too few rows to pin them down.\n",
        "\n",
        "Ridge, with the *same* features and the *same* 300 rows, gives up a little training fit and lands within $1,000 of the full-data OLS. That is what a penalty buys — and it buys the most exactly when the data runs out."
      ],
      "id": "cHQyui4sx8sH"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "Ml8pZfilx8sH"
      },
      "source": [
        "### 7 · Ridge and lasso — a penalty on the coefficients\n",
        "\n",
        "OLS minimises the squared error. Ridge and lasso minimise the squared error **plus a price for large coefficients:**\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\\text{ridge:}\\quad \\sum_i (y_i - \\mathbf{x}_i\\boldsymbol\\beta)^2 \\;+\\; \\lambda \\sum_j \\beta_j^2\n",
        "\\qquad\\qquad\n",
        "\\text{lasso:}\\quad \\sum_i (y_i - \\mathbf{x}_i\\boldsymbol\\beta)^2 \\;+\\; \\lambda \\sum_j \\lvert\\beta_j\\rvert$$\n",
        "\n",
        "- $\\lambda = 0$ is OLS. Larger $\\lambda$ shrinks every coefficient toward zero.\n",
        "- **Ridge shrinks.** Coefficients get small, never exactly zero.\n",
        "- **Lasso selects.** The absolute-value penalty pushes weak coefficients *exactly* to zero — the model drops features by itself.\n",
        "- **Elastic net** mixes the two penalties. One slider, both behaviours.\n",
        "\n",
        "The economist's reading: a penalty is a prior that effects are small. With few rows and many features, that prior is worth more than the data's own opinion."
      ],
      "id": "Ml8pZfilx8sH"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "Y58vyHqIx8sH"
      },
      "source": [
        "### Scale first, or the penalty is unfair\n",
        "\n",
        "The penalty charges every coefficient the same price. But a coefficient's size depends on its feature's units: `median_income` runs 0.5–15, `population` runs into the thousands. Unscaled, the penalty crushes whichever feature happens to have big numbers.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Standardise every numeric feature before penalising.** Trees won't care about this. Penalised linear models care about nothing else."
      ],
      "id": "Y58vyHqIx8sH"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
