      "source": [
        "### Features beat algorithms — when you do the work\n",
        "\n",
        "On Ames, **plain OLS beats the forest and the booster.** Same models, opposite verdict.\n",
        "\n",
        "The difference is not the algorithm. It is 215 features that two sessions of human judgment built: *which* quality scales are ordinal, *which* columns to log, *what* a missing garage year means. Once the features carry the structure, a line is enough — and a line is faster, more stable, and you can read its coefficients.\n",
        "\n",
        "- **Do nothing to the features → ensembles win big** (California: a third less error).\n",
        "- **Do the work → the line catches up or wins** (Ames).\n",
        "\n",
        "Which one you should do depends on what the prediction is for, how much time you have, and whether anyone needs to explain the model afterwards. That is a judgment, and it's yours."
      ],
      "id": "l8M0F8QAx8sK"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "hiMECvImx8sK"
      },
      "source": [
        "### What to take away\n",
        "\n",
        "1. **Judge a number in its own units.** MAE in dollars first; R² for comparison across datasets.\n",
        "2. **Linear models need the preprocessing; trees don't.** Impute, encode, log, scale — for the line. The forest ran on the raw columns.\n",
        "3. **A penalty is a prior that effects are small.** Ridge shrinks, lasso selects, and the penalty must be tuned by cross-validation on scaled features.\n",
        "4. **One tree memorises. Many trees generalise.** Forests average; boosting corrects. Both beat the line by a third here with no tuning at all.\n",
        "5. **Open the box with permutation importance and partial dependence.** They work for any model — and they told us *why* the line lost.\n",
        "6. **Features versus algorithms is a trade-off, not a verdict.** Know which side of it your problem is on.\n",
        "\n",
        "*Project 3: your turn, on a dataset where you will have to decide.*"
      ],
